\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Left Clifford multiplication is not the commutator action}

\p{Let #{\mathfrak g} be a finite-dimensional complex semisimple Lie
algebra with Killing form #{\kappa}, and put
#{Q_\kappa(x)=\kappa(x,x)}. If #{(b_i)} is a basis and
#{(b_i^\vee)} its Killing-dual basis, the normalized quadratic lift of
the adjoint action is

##{\delta(x)=\frac14\sum_i
\beta_{Q_\kappa}([x,b_i],b_i^\vee)\in\Cl(Q_\kappa)^0.}

The coefficient is the one derived in \ref{fcap-0020}: the polar form is
#{B_{Q_\kappa}=2\kappa}, so the polar-dual basis contributes one factor
#{1/2}, and the general lift contributes the other. This is a grade-two
element, not the canonical generator #{\iota_{Q_\kappa}(x)}.}

\p{There are two natural actions on the same Clifford vector space:

##{L_x(c)=\delta(x)c,\qquad
D_x(c)=\delta(x)c-c\delta(x).}

If #{R_a(c)=ca}, then

##{D_x=L_x-R_{\delta(x)},\qquad [L_x,R_a]=0.}

Under equivariant Clifford antisymmetrization,
#{D} is the derivation extension of the adjoint action to
#{\bigwedge\mathfrak g}. The action #{L} is instead left multiplication
on the Clifford algebra. On matrix coordinates
#{\operatorname{End}(S)\simeq S\otimes S^*}, it acts on the #{S} factor
and leaves #{S^*} as a multiplicity space.}

\p{TauCeti defines the selected left action and proves these comparisons
in
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.kostantLieModule}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.kostant_lie_def}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.kostant_lie_sub_mul}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.kostantRightMul}.
The generic module lift in \ref{fcap-001O} supplies the same algebraic
mechanism, but it does not identify the left action with the exterior
derivation action.}

\p{\citet{Sections 3.1 and 5.2, pp. 294 and 311}{kostant1997clifford}
defines the left action through this quarter-normalized lift and
separately identifies the commutator with the exterior extension.
\citet{Section 2, internal preprint pp. 7--8}{panyushev2001exterior}
studies the exterior/spin action under the same complex semisimple
hypotheses. The following example shows why their roles cannot be
exchanged.}
